/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Petrol needs about 14.7 kg of air for every 1 kg of fuel to burn completely.
   - AFR is the actual ratio in kg of air per kg of petrol; AFRst = 14.7 for petrol; λ has no unit. λ = 1 is stoichiometric, λ < 1 is rich (too little air for the fuel) and λ > 1 is lean (excess air).
   - Each pollutant follows λ in its own way.
   - A catalytic converter is a ceramic or metal honeycomb in the exhaust, coated with a porous washcoat that carries precious metals (platinum and palladium for oxidation, rhodium for NOx reduction).
   - A three-way catalyst does both jobs at once, but only in a narrow window around λ = 1, about 1 percent wide.
   - The catalyst must also be hot.
   - EGR, exhaust gas recirculation, sends a metered part of the exhaust back to the intake.
   - Spark timing also moves the emissions.
   - Fuel economy has two common forms. km per litre is higher-is-better; litres per 100 km is lower-is-better.
   - CO₂ is not a product of bad combustion: almost all the carbon in the fuel leaves as CO₂ when the burn is good.
*/
