/* book Worked example 2: Q: On the test cycle a petrol car makes, before the catalyst, CO 8.0 g/km, HC 1.2 g/km and NOx 1.0 g/km. With the catalyst hot and λ held at 1, conversion is CO 90 %, HC 92 %, NOx 95 %. (a) Find the tailpipe values and compare them with BS VI (CO 1.0, HC 0.10, NOx 0.06 g/km). (b) A faulty sensor makes the engine run at λ = 1.02, where NOx conversion falls to 60 %. Find the new tailpipe NOx.
   1. Tailpipe = engine-out × (1 − conversion).
   2. CO: 8.0 × (1 − 0.90) = 0.80 g/km, under 1.0. HC: 1.2 × (1 − 0.92) = 0.096 g/km, under 0.10. NOx: 1.0 × (1 − 0.95) = 0.05 g/km, under 0.06. The car passes, with HC only 4 % under its ceiling.
   3. At λ = 1.02 the spare oxygen stops the NOx reduction: NOx = 1.0 × (1 − 0.60) = 0.40 g/km.
   4. 0.40 ÷ 0.06 = 6.7 times the limit. A 2 % lean drift fails the car, which is why the ECU holds λ = 1 in closed loop.
   Answer: Tailpipe CO 0.80, HC 0.096, NOx 0.05 g/km: a pass. At λ = 1.02 NOx rises to 0.40 g/km, 6.7 times the BS VI limit. */
